-- Passage rg8-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9d669a71-47e6-5bb8-981f-6a86d8c0dad4', 'rg8-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Leaves Change Color$q$, $q$Every autumn, maples and oaks in many parts of the country trade their green leaves for shades of red, orange, and yellow. The change can look like magic, but it follows a clear chain of causes inside each leaf.

During spring and summer, leaves are packed with chlorophyll, a green chemical that captures sunlight to make food for the tree. Chlorophyll is so plentiful that its green color hides other colors in the leaf. Yellow and orange pigments are there all summer long. They are simply covered up.

As autumn arrives, days grow shorter and nights grow cooler. The tree responds to these signals by slowly sealing off each leaf where its stem meets the branch. With its supply line cut, the leaf stops making new chlorophyll. The old chlorophyll breaks down, and as the green fades, the hidden yellows and oranges finally show.

Red is different. Unlike yellow and orange, red pigments are not present all summer. Some trees produce them in autumn, especially after sunny days and cool nights. Scientists think the red pigments may protect the leaf while the tree pulls useful nutrients back into its branches.

Weather therefore shapes each year's colors. A dry, cloudy autumn may bring dull shades, while bright days and crisp nights can turn whole hillsides scarlet.$q$, 'draft')
